source_questions.source_raw:
QUESTION 2 (10 points) At time t = 0, a particle is represented by the wave function:
\[
\Psi(x,0) =
\begin{cases}
A(x/a), & \text{if } 0 \le x \le a\\
A(b-x)/(b-a), & \text{if } a \le x \le b\\
0, & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\]
where $A = \frac{3}{b}$, and $a$ and $b$ are (positive) constants. What is the probability of finding the particle to the left of $a$?

source_questions.stem:
At time $t=0$, a particle is represented by the wave function:
\[
\Psi(x,0) =
\begin{cases}
A(x/a), & \text{if } 0 \le x \le a\\
A(b-x)/(b-a), & \text{if } a \le x \le b\\
0, & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\]
where $A = \frac{3}{b}$, and $a$ and $b$ are (positive) constants. What is the probability of finding the particle to the left of $a$?

The infinite square well potential is given by:
\[
V(x) =
\begin{cases}
0, & \text{if } 0 \le x \le a\\
\infty, & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\]

